out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Rizk has counted the reasons in her refusals and the appeals against them: an average of two point seven reasons, thirty-one refusals, seven appealed, and the seven that were appealed averaged three point four reasons, while the single-reason letters were appealed twice, and both of those appeals were about the reason itself, which is the argument she wants. Mrs Khoury asked about the appeals against the three-reason letters, and five of the seven went at the weakest reason and ignored the other two, and one of them was right, which is the whole problem, because when the weakest reason falls the letter looks as though it was built out of whatever came to hand. Mr Attar asked why the strong reasons would not survive, and Mrs Khoury said they survive and the letter does not: two reasons imply that neither was sufficient on its own, which is what putting two of them in a letter says whether you meant it or not. Mrs Oueidat asked why anybody writes three, and because one reason feels thin on the page, and that is a feeling about the writer and not about the letter: Mrs Rizk has added a second reason to twenty-one letters this year and in nineteen of them the first reason was sufficient. Dr Qabbani said that is a tidy pattern, and Mrs Khoury said it is the pattern: the strong reason is a fact about the world and the second reason is a fault in the application. Mr Shihabi said a short refusal reads as careless, and Mrs Oueidat said it reads as careless to the writer and as clear to the reader, and the room has it the wrong way round. Mr Tayara asked what happens if there genuinely are two independent reasons, and then you choose, and the choice is the unremovable one. Mrs Tarabishi asked whether anything may go in beside the reason, and one thing, and it is not a reason: the free sentence from the cycle before. And Mrs Ajouz asked about in any case, and Mrs Rizk said it is the two words that give the whole thing away and she has written them eleven times this year. Mrs Oueidat said four students in the class have received one-reason refusals, and all four knew what to do next and none of them called the letter careless, while three of the four described the three-reason refusals they had also received as evasive, which nobody writing one intends. And if both reasons are unremovable, the choice is the one the reader can act on, and the other is not mentioned, since it will still be true next year.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **One figure in the report does more work than the rest and it is the nineteen.** **Twenty-one letters with a second reason, and in nineteen the first reason was sufficient** — **and she knows which nineteen because the first reasons are facts about the world and the second reasons are faults in applications.** **That is a sorting rule the room can apply to its own letters in a minute, and it should be applied before the lesson ends.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **The second reason put into the silence** → **six of ten; the cure is a timetable and not courage.**   **2** **_In any case_** → **eleven times in one officer\'s year.**   **3** ⚠ **The weakest reason kept because it is the most defensible in conversation** → **it is the one that falls.**   **4** **Two reasons given as a pair** → **each implies the other was not enough.**   **5** ⚠ **The free sentence counted as a second reason** → **it is a fact and gives nothing to argue with.**   **6** **The one-reason letter padded with courtesy** → **the courtesy reads as a second reason.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **The timetable objection was based on a dry-season date the governorate had moved in July, which she did not know.** **Once it fell, the committee had to defend two reasons that nobody had challenged** — **in front of a board that had just been shown the programme was wrong about something, which is the cost and is not recoverable.**'));
out.push(A('**2** ⚠ **_If the district rule were enough, why was the timetable mentioned?_** **The appellant\'s own sentence is the argument of the whole cycle** — **and it was available to him only because the letter had given him two reasons.**'));
out.push(A('**3** ⚠ **She writes the reason, leaves the paragraph, and comes back to it in the afternoon.** **It is a cure for the silence rather than for the letter** — **and the note is worth more to another officer for that one mechanical instruction than for any of its figures.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
